WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.537
Iterator defects often appear only under boundary conditions or specific mutation patterns.

2
00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:09.831
A loop may work for a typical container and fail for an empty one.

3
00:00:09.831 --> 00:00:15.125
It may process a middle element correctly but mishandle the first or last element.

4
00:00:15.125 --> 00:00:19.284
It may pass tests until insertion causes a vector to reallocate.

5
00:00:19.284 --> 00:00:26.468
Tests should cover empty ranges, single-element ranges, end conditions, erase while iterating, insertion during traversal, container growth, clear

6
00:00:26.468 --> 00:00:29.493
operations, stored iterators, view lifetimes, and shutdown paths.

7
00:00:29.493 --> 00:00:37.434
Mutation-heavy code deserves direct tests because the risk is usually in the transition from one valid state to the next.

8
00:00:37.434 --> 00:00:42.728
Debug iterator modes in standard library implementations can detect many invalid operations during development.

9
00:00:42.728 --> 00:00:50.290
They are not a substitute for understanding the rules, but they provide valuable feedback when code compares unrelated iterators, dereferences

10
00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:53.693
invalid positions, or uses iterators after a container change.

11
00:00:53.693 --> 00:01:00.121
These builds may be slower, which is why they are often used in development or CI configurations.

12
00:01:00.121 --> 00:01:05.037
Sanitizers can reveal related memory access problems, lifetime issues, and undefined behavior symptoms.

13
00:01:05.037 --> 00:01:11.086
Compiler warnings, static analysis, assertions, logging, and crash diagnostics can also point to suspicious traversal patterns.

14
00:01:11.086 --> 00:01:16.380
The strongest approach layers these tools instead of expecting one diagnostic to catch everything.

15
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:22.808
When a tool finds an iterator defect, treat it as an engineering defect, not a flaky test.

16
00:01:22.808 --> 00:01:28.480
Capture the boundary condition, add regression coverage, and review nearby code for the same pattern.
